# Testing

Howdy apps are tested by sending them requests. The `howdy/testing` module
builds a request, runs it through your app, and hands back the response,
all without starting a server. Nothing touches a socket, so tests are fast,
need no ports, and can run side by side.

The whole pipeline runs, exactly as it would for a real request: routing,
middleware, guards, versioning, body decoding and validation. A test that
passes here is a good promise about how the running app behaves.

## Make the app testable

Tests need to build your app, so keep the app separate from `main`. Let
`main` do the things only a running program needs, such as reading
configuration, configuring logging and starting the server, and put
everything else in a function that returns the app:

```gleam
import howdy

pub fn app() {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.middleware(logger.log)
  |> howdy.controller(user_controller())
}

pub fn main() {
  logging.configure()
  let assert Ok(_) = howdy.start(app())
  process.sleep_forever()
}
```

If the app needs something from outside, such as a database connection, take
it as an argument: `app(db)`. Tests can then pass in one made for the test.

## A first test

Howdy projects use `gleeunit`, which runs every public function whose name
ends in `_test` when you run `gleam test`:

```gleam
import gleam/json
import howdy/testing
import my_app

pub fn create_user_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post("/users", json.object([#("name", json.string("Ada"))]))
    |> testing.send(my_app.app())

  assert res.status == 201
}
```

Every test has the same three steps: build a request, `send` it to the app,
and assert on the response.

## Building requests

Start with a function for the method:

- `testing.get(path)` and `testing.delete(path)` have no body.
- `testing.post`, `put` and `patch` take a path and a JSON body, and set the
  JSON content type.
- `testing.post_form(path, pairs)` posts an HTML form as a browser would:
  the name and value pairs are encoded, and the form content type is set.
- `testing.post_multipart(path, pairs, files)` posts a form with files as
  `multipart/form-data`, each file a `testing.FormFile`.
- `testing.request(method, path)` covers everything else, such as `HEAD`
  and `OPTIONS`, or a `POST` with no body.

A query string written in the path is kept, so `testing.get("/users?page=2")`
does what you would expect.

Then adjust the request by piping it through any of these:

- `header(name, value)` sets a header.
- `query(pairs)` sets the query string from name and value pairs, encoding
  the values for you. It replaces any query already there.
- `cookie(name, value)` adds a cookie, encoded the way `cookie.set` writes
  them.
- `json_body(json)`, `form_body(pairs)`, `multipart_body(pairs, files)`,
  `text_body(string)` and `bytes_body(bits)` replace the body. All but the last set a matching
  content type; to send a different one, set the header afterwards.
- `from_ip(address)` makes the request appear to come from an address.
  Without it the client address is unknown, and `rate_limit.by_ip` does not
  count the request.

```gleam
let res =
  testing.get("/users")
  |> testing.query([#("search", "ada lovelace")])
  |> testing.header("x-api-key", "secret")
  |> testing.cookie("session", "abc")
  |> testing.send(my_app.app())
```

A test request is an ordinary `gleam/http/request` value, so the functions
in that module work on it too.

## Reading responses

A response is an ordinary `gleam/http/response` value. Read the status and
headers from it directly:

```gleam
import gleam/http/response

assert res.status == 200
assert response.get_header(res, "content-type")
  == Ok("application/json; charset=utf-8")
```

For the body, and the things Howdy puts in it, there are helpers:

- `testing.text(res)` is the body as a string. An empty body is `""`.
- `testing.bytes(res)` is the body as a `BitArray`.
- `testing.json(res, decoder)` decodes a JSON body with a decoder from
  `gleam/dynamic/decode`.
- `testing.error(res)` is the message from a Howdy
  [error response](/docs/controllers/services), the `error` in
  `{"error":"..."}`.
- `testing.field_errors(res)` is the list of field errors from a `422`.
- `testing.cookies(res)` is every cookie the response sets, as name and
  decoded value pairs. A deleted cookie has an empty value.
- `testing.open_events(res)` runs a
  [server-sent event](/docs/controllers/server-sent-events) stream without
  a socket. Read what it sends with `testing.next_event` and end it with
  `testing.close_events`.

Prefer `testing.json` to comparing body text. A test that decodes the body
into a typed value keeps passing when the order of the fields changes:

```gleam
pub fn show_user_test() {
  let res = testing.get("/users/1") |> testing.send(my_app.app())

  assert res.status == 200
  assert testing.json(res, user_decoder()) == Ok(User(id: 1, name: "Ada"))
}
```

For errors, assert on the status and the message:

```gleam
import howdy/service.{FieldError}

pub fn blank_name_is_rejected_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post("/users", json.object([#("name", json.string(""))]))
    |> testing.send(my_app.app())

  assert res.status == 422
  assert testing.field_errors(res)
    == Ok([FieldError("name", "must not be empty")])
}
```

## What to test where

Send requests to test what only a request can show: that a route exists,
that the guard protects it, that bad input gets the right status, that the
response has the right shape. One or two tests per route is often enough.

Test the rules themselves as plain functions. A validator, a service or a
decoder takes a value and returns a value, so there is no need to go through
HTTP, and the tests are shorter for it:

```gleam
pub fn name_is_trimmed_test() {
  let assert Ok(user) = validate_new_user(NewUserInput(name: "  Ada "))
  assert user.name == "Ada"
}
```

To check your routes without running any handlers, `router.match` does the
matching on its own. See [Router](/docs/controllers/router).

## Keeping tests independent

Each test should build what it needs and leave nothing behind, so that tests
can run in any order, and at the same time.

Build a fresh app in each test, not one shared between them. That matters
most for anything that keeps state in the app, such as a
[rate limiter](/docs/middleware/rate-limiting), whose counts would carry
over from one test to the next.

For a database, give each test its own. A helper that takes the test as a
function can create an in-memory database, run the migrations, run the test
and close the database afterwards:

```gleam
pub fn list_todos_test() {
  use db <- support.with_db()
  let res = testing.get("/api/todos") |> testing.send(my_app.app(db))
  assert res.status == 200
}
```

A failed assertion crashes the test, so a plain call after `run_test` would
never close the database of a test that failed. `testing.cleanup` runs its
function either way, and the test still fails:

```gleam
pub fn with_db(run_test: fn(Repo) -> a) -> a {
  let assert Ok(db) = sqlite.start(sqlite.memory())
  use <- testing.cleanup(fn() { repo.close(db) })
  let assert Ok(_) = database.migrate(db)
  run_test(db)
}
```

Create test data through the same validators and services your handlers
use, not by writing rows by hand. Fixtures built that way cannot drift out
of step with the rules of the app.

## What testing cannot do

- There is no connection to upgrade, so a
  [WebSocket](/docs/controllers/websockets) route answers `426` once it
  reaches `upgrade`. Everything in front of it, including the guard, the
  parameters and the origin check, is tested as normal.
- `testing.text` and `testing.bytes` read a body from `howdy/stream` in
  full, but cannot read a file from `howdy/static`, and panic if asked to.
  Check the status and headers of those responses instead.
- Nothing is sent over the network, so the server's own behaviour is not
  covered: timeouts, TLS, HTTP/2. Start the app with `howdy.start` on
  port `0` and read the chosen port from `started.data.port` to test those.

